-- title:
--   Sequential compactness of the place space of a complex function field
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{C}$-algebra structure, and assume there is an element $x \in F$ transcendental over $\mathbb{C}$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $\mathbb{C}(x)$ generated by $x$. Assume moreover `IsCurveOver ℂ F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ admits a divisor of degree $0$ whose value at each place is $\operatorname{ord}_v f$, each residue field $\kappa(v)$ is a finite-dimensional $\mathbb{C}$-module, and the module of Kähler differentials $\Omega[F/\mathbb{C}]$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$. Here a place of $F/\mathbb{C}$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\mathbb{C}$, different from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring; $\operatorname{ord}_v f$ is minus the logarithm of the associated height-one-spectrum valuation of $f$, and $v.\mathrm{evalAt}\, f \in \mathbb{C}$ is the residue of $f$ at $v$ read back through $\mathbb{C} \to \kappa(v)$ when $f$ lies in the valuation subring, and $0$ otherwise. Then for every sequence $(w_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of places of $F/\mathbb{C}$ there are a place $v$ and a strictly increasing $\varphi : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ such that for every $f \in F$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v f \ge 0$ one has $\operatorname{ord}_{w_{\varphi(n)}} f \ge 0$ for all sufficiently large $n$, and $w_{\varphi(n)}.\mathrm{evalAt}\, f \to v.\mathrm{evalAt}\, f$ in $\mathbb{C}$.
--
--   This is the compactness of the Riemann surface attached to an algebraic function field of one variable over $\mathbb{C}$, expressed intrinsically as sequential compactness of the set of places for the topology of pointwise convergence of values of functions regular at the limit place. It feeds the statement [`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_tendsto_appLE_of_isProper_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one_complex`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.exists_tendsto_appLE_of_isProper_of_smoothOfRelativeDimension_one_complex), where convergence of sections along a sequence of points of a proper smooth complex curve is needed.
